+### Conditional writes
+
+`PutObject`, `CopyObject`, and `CompleteMultipartUpload` support conditional
+writes to the destination:
+
+- `If-None-Match: *` creates an object only if the key does not already exist.
+- `If-Match: "ETag"` replaces an object only if its current ETag matches.
+  Use the ETag returned by `HEAD` or `GET`. `If-Match: *` requires an existing
+  object without comparing its contents.
+
+A failed condition returns `412 PreconditionFailed`, except that `If-Match`
+on a missing object returns `404 NoSuchKey`. Only a single strong ETag is
+accepted; empty conditions, weak ETags and ETag lists return `400 InvalidArgument`.
+For multipart uploads the condition is checked at completion, not initiation.
+A rejected completion leaves the upload available to retry or abort.
+
+The check and write are serialized with other writes, copies, deletes and
+multipart completions for the same key through this server and VFS. Unrelated
+keys can still be written concurrently. This does not coordinate separate
+server instances, overlapping auth-proxy mappings, other rclone commands,
+or changes made directly to the backing remote. All writers participating
+in compare-and-swap must use the same server and VFS, and distinct keys
+must not alias one backing object.
+
+On Linux with a local XFS backend and VFS caching below `minimal`, conditional
+plain PUT commits use `XFS_IOC_START_COMMIT` and `XFS_IOC_COMMIT_RANGE` when
+the running kernel supports them. XFS rechecks destination freshness at the
+atomic range exchange, so a destination changed outside this server causes
+the commit to fail instead of silently overwriting the newer contents. Other
+filesystems, older kernels and cached VFS writes use the normal serialized
+rename path.
+
+Conditional writes require a backend with server-side move or copy, so that
+multipart uploads can be staged without changing the destination before
+completion. Unsupported backends return `501 NotImplemented`; do not use
+conditional multipart uploads on them. Comparing an ETag also returns `501`
+if the configured hash is disabled or unavailable. Create-if-absent and
+`If-Match: *` do not need a hash.
+
+ETags identify contents, not a unique revision: restoring the same contents
+can restore the same ETag. Include a revision or nonce in the object when
+this distinction matters. Conditional writes do not change VFS write-back
+or the durability guarantees of the backing remote.
+
